invok — installer for Odoo, with surgical rollback
==================================================

You are reading this from an installed package, so this file does not
explain how to install invok: you already have it. It says what you
have, where an installation puts things, and how to remove it.

The full documentation — the engine, the step-by-step reference, the
rollback model, multi-distribution support — lives in the wiki:

    https://github.com/Omisen/invok/wiki


THE COMMAND
-----------

    invok             install Odoo, guided by prompts
    invok --dry-run   resolve the configuration and print the plan,
                      changing nothing on the system
    invok list        the instances installed on this machine
    invok --help      every option
    invok -V          the installer's own version

`vok` is a symlink to the same program, not a second binary:
`vok --dry-run` and `invok --dry-run` do exactly the same thing.

invok runs through sudo, from a normal user:

    sudo invok

Either the installation succeeds completely, or the system goes back to
exactly what it was. There is no intermediate state left behind.


MORE THAN ONE ODOO ON THE SAME MACHINE
--------------------------------------

An instance is created by NAMING it. Re-running the installer does not
add one: without a name it targets the installation already there, and
stops rather than overwrite it.

    sudo invok --instance cliente-x --port 8169

The name qualifies everything that instance owns — system user,
PostgreSQL role, database, service unit, directory, config file — so
two instances share nothing but the machine. --port moves the pair of
ports it needs (HTTP, and longpolling three above it); a port another
instance has recorded is refused even when nothing is listening on it.

In a .env file the same choice is ODOO_INSTANCE.


WHAT AN INSTALLATION LEAVES ON THE MACHINE
------------------------------------------

    /opt/odoo/odoo<version>        sources, virtualenv, config file
    /opt/odoo/.local/share/Odoo    the filestore (attachments)
    /etc/systemd/system/           the odoo<version> service unit
    /var/lib/invok/state.json      the uninstall manifest (root, 0600)
    /var/log/invok.log             the installer's own log
    /run/invok.lock                the lock, while a run is in progress

A NAMED instance keeps the same shape under its own name:

    /opt/odoo/odoo-<name>                     everything of its own,
                                              filestore included
    /etc/systemd/system/odoo-<name>.service   its unit
    /var/lib/invok/instances/<name>.json      its manifest

With --with-nginx, a virtual host and the firewall rules for it.

The manifest is the only record of which artifacts invok created and
which ones it found already on the machine. Do not remove it: it is
what makes the installation removable later.


REMOVING AN INSTALLATION
------------------------

    sudo invok rollback --dry-run          list what would be undone
    sudo invok rollback                    undo it
    sudo invok rollback --instance <name>  undo that one
    sudo invok rollback --all              undo every instance

Only what invok created is removed. A database, a package, a user or a
configuration file that was already on the machine is left untouched —
that distinction is the whole point of the manifest.

With more than one instance installed, removing one leaves whatever the
others are standing on: /opt/odoo, the system packages, the PostgreSQL
cluster, nginx. The report names what it left and why, and that
instance's manifest is kept as the record of who owns those — so the
last removal can still take them away.

If an installation is interrupted, the same command cleans up whatever
that run had already done.


